**What DNS Amplification Is**
DNS (Domain Name System) is the internet's phonebook. Each domain name resolves to an IP address. When you type "example.com" into your browser, your device looks up the DNS record for that domain. The record tells you the server responding to your query (often called a resolver).

**How Amplification Works**
When a resolver receives a DNS query and finds an authoritative server, it returns the cached response back to the client. It doesn't need to ask the authoritative server for every query — it can simply return the cached response and let the client do the work of contacting the actual server.

**Why It's Dangerous**
If you control one resolver, you can make a recursive query that hits hundreds of other authoritative servers, effectively piggybacking on their connections. This is how large-scale attacks work — the attacker sends thousands of small requests to overwhelm servers.

**How to Protect Against It**
Use platforms like Cloudflare Workers, Cloudflare DNS, or Cloudflare Pages to cache responses. For local development, configure the resolver as a caching proxy. Keep your resolvers updated and route all traffic through them.

**How to Use It**
You don't need special modules. In your Angular app, you configure a resolver provider. In your browser, configure a DNS resolver. The dashboard shows you how many requests are being served from your provider vs those you're hitting yourself.

